{
  "turn": 12,
  "input": "Iris, I leave room for your answer. What would you want me to understand before we choose how to spend this afternoon?",
  "first_physical": 29,
  "last_physical": 31,
  "outcome": {
    "ok": true,
    "turn_id": "d09_unique_value:r16:t4",
    "narration": "Iris is quiet for a moment. The sparse room offers little to fill the silence — bare wardrobes, empty cabinets, two beds with their stools and nightstands. Light from the tower's narrow windows falls across the stone floor in pale strips.\n\nIris shifts her weight, then brushes a thumb across her own knuckles. \"You've asked me a lot of hard questions in a row,\" she says. \"I haven't answered most of them yet.\" There's no sharpness in it, but no apology either — just an observation, plainly stated.\n\nShe looks toward the empty medical-storage cabinets for a moment, then back. \"Before we decide anything about this afternoon — I'd want you to understand that I don't owe you answers just because we're standing in your tower. I'll answer what I can, when I can. That's the only honest way I know how to do this.\" She pauses, and something in her expression softens, though not into deference. \"Ask me one at a time, and I'll try. That's what I'd want from you too.\"",
